{
  "image_name": "1d0d44143a3ee16254db6c2aa58c689f.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a healthy intestine with an inflamed one, emphasizing how the mucus barrier, microbiota, and immune responses change. In the healthy state, intestinal epithelial cells are covered by distinct inner and outer mucus layers that keep bacteria at a distance. Protective factors such as sIgA and β-defensin are shown in the mucus, while immune cells beneath the epithelium are associated with anti-inflammatory signals like IL-10 and TGF-β. Dietary components are converted by resident bacteria into metabolic products, including short-chain fatty acids, suggesting a stable, beneficial host–microbe relationship.\n\nIn the inflamed state, this organization appears disrupted. The mucus barrier is altered, an ulcer is present, and bacteria are shown much closer to or crossing the epithelium. A Paneth cell is highlighted near the damaged area, and labels indicate bacterial translocation and TLR ligands reaching underlying tissue. Immune cells are more numerous and linked to pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IFN-γ, IL-17, TNF, IL-1β, IL-6, IL-8, and IL-12. The center label “dysbiosis” and the taxonomic lists at the bottom suggest that both luminal and mucus-associated bacterial communities shift during inflammation, accompanying loss of barrier function and heightened immune activation.",
